# Property reads through Req and the runtime over a real socket

## Goal

Read a Property of an HTTP Thing twice: through `Wotex.Runtime.ConsumedThing`
with the HTTP binding, and directly through the Lab's `Req` client that
implements the binding's client port. A disposable Bandit server started in
this notebook answers on a loopback port, so every exchange crosses a real
socket, and the failure cells show how oversized, slow, redirected and
mistyped responses become classed runtime errors instead of values.

## Prerequisites

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:wotex_lab, "~> 0.1.0"},
  {:req, "~> 0.7.4"},
  {:bandit, "~> 1.12"},
  {:plug, "~> 1.18"}
])
```

The requirements above are published-artifact requirements. At the time this
notebook was written none of the `wotex*` packages is published on Hex; the
notebook is verified against the workspace source cohort recorded in
`docs/provenance/source-cohort.json` by `test/wotex/lab/cookbook_test.exs`,
which evaluates every Elixir cell below in order (skipping this install
cell). `Mix.install/1` never points at a Git checkout. `req` is an optional
dependency of the Lab that a host selects; `bandit` and `plug` serve only the
disposable server defined below.

## Package and consumer ownership

| Concern | Owner | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Form selection, request identity, deadlines, result and error shape | `wotex_runtime` (package-implements) | `Wotex.Runtime.ConsumedThing` |
| HTTP request/response values, status and media-type mapping | `wotex_binding_http` (package-implements) | `Wotex.Binding.HTTP` |
| The HTTP client that moves bytes | `wotex_lab` (consumer-implements `Wotex.Binding.HTTP.Client`) | `Wotex.Lab.Adapters.HTTP.ReqClient` |
| Redirects | `wotex_lab` client: refused (`redirect: false`), reported as an HTTP status | `ReqClient.request/3` |
| The server | this notebook | `WotexLabCookbook.ConsumeHttpServer` |

## Run

```elixir
alias Wotex.Binding.HTTP
alias Wotex.Binding.HTTP.Request, as: HTTPRequest
alias Wotex.Binding.HTTP.Response, as: HTTPResponse
alias Wotex.Lab.Adapters.HTTP.ReqClient
alias Wotex.Lab.Adapters.Runtime.NoSec
alias Wotex.Lab.Telemetry
alias Wotex.Runtime.{ConsumedThing, Context, Error, Result}
alias Wotex.ThingDescription

telemetry_id = "cookbook-consume-http-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"

events =
  for component <- Telemetry.components(),
      operation <- Telemetry.operations(),
      event <- [:start, :stop, :exception],
      do: [:wotex, :lab, component, operation, event]

:ok = :telemetry.attach_many(telemetry_id, events, &Telemetry.forward/4, self())
```

The disposable server. Each route matches one Property of the fixture TD,
including the ones that misbehave on purpose.

```elixir
defmodule WotexLabCookbook.ConsumeHttpServer do
  use Plug.Router

  plug(:match)
  plug(:dispatch)

  get "/properties/temperature" do
    conn |> put_resp_content_type("application/json") |> send_resp(200, "21.5")
  end

  get "/properties/large" do
    body = "\"" <> String.duplicate("x", 4_096) <> "\""
    conn |> put_resp_content_type("application/json") |> send_resp(200, body)
  end

  get "/properties/slow" do
    Process.sleep(400)
    conn |> put_resp_content_type("application/json") |> send_resp(200, "1")
  end

  get "/properties/redirect" do
    conn |> put_resp_header("location", "/properties/temperature") |> send_resp(302, "")
  end

  get "/properties/html" do
    conn |> put_resp_content_type("text/html") |> send_resp(200, "<b>21</b>")
  end

  match _ do
    send_resp(conn, 404, "")
  end
end

{:ok, server} =
  Bandit.start_link(
    plug: WotexLabCookbook.ConsumeHttpServer,
    ip: {127, 0, 0, 1},
    port: 0,
    startup_log: false
  )

{:ok, {_ip, port}} = ThousandIsland.listener_info(server)
base = "http://127.0.0.1:#{port}/"
```

The fixture TD carries a placeholder `base`; point it at the server and build
a `ConsumedThing` with the HTTP profile, the Lab client and a `nosec`
credential port.

```elixir
fixture = Application.app_dir(:wotex_lab, "priv/fixtures/http/thing-description.json")
{:ok, fixture_td} = fixture |> File.read!() |> ThingDescription.parse()
{:ok, td} = fixture_td |> ThingDescription.to_map() |> Map.put("base", base) |> ThingDescription.from_map()

{:ok, profile} = HTTP.profile()

{:ok, config} =
  HTTP.config(client: {ReqClient, %{receive_timeout: 2_000}}, max_response_bytes: 1_024)

{:ok, consumed} =
  ConsumedThing.new(td,
    profiles: [profile],
    transports: %{profile.id => HTTP.transport(config)},
    credentials: {NoSec, []}
  )

ThingDescription.id(td)
```

Read through the runtime: the result carries the operation, the request id,
the decoded payload and the HTTP status in its metadata.

```elixir
context =
  Context.new!(request_id: "http-read", deadline: System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) + 2_000)

{:ok, %Result{} = runtime_result} = ConsumedThing.read_property(consumed, "temperature", context)
{runtime_result.operation, runtime_result.status, runtime_result.payload, runtime_result.metadata.http.status}
```

The same bytes through the client port alone. A consumer that only needs
HTTP values can stop here; the runtime adds selection, identity and retry
classes on top.

```elixir
{:ok, request} =
  HTTPRequest.new("GET", base <> "properties/temperature", [{"accept", "application/json"}], nil,
    request_id: "http-direct",
    operation: :readproperty,
    media_type: "application/json",
    stream?: false,
    deadline: nil,
    max_response_bytes: 1_024,
    max_event_bytes: 64
  )

{:ok, %HTTPResponse{status: direct_status} = direct} =
  Telemetry.span(:http, :request, %{profile: :cookbook_direct}, fn ->
    ReqClient.request(request, nil, %{})
  end)

{direct_status, direct.body}
```

## Deliberate breakage

Each misbehaving Property produces a runtime error with a retry `class` and
a `cause` from the binding; none of them becomes a payload.

```elixir
{:error, %Error{class: oversized_class} = oversized} =
  ConsumedThing.read_property(consumed, "large", context)

quick = Context.new!(request_id: "http-slow", deadline: System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) + 100)
{:error, %Error{class: slow_class}} = ConsumedThing.read_property(consumed, "slow", quick)

{:error, %Error{class: redirect_class} = redirect} =
  ConsumedThing.read_property(consumed, "redirect", context)

{:error, %Error{class: html_class} = html} = ConsumedThing.read_property(consumed, "html", context)

%{
  oversized: {oversized_class, oversized.details.cause.code},
  slow: slow_class,
  redirect: {redirect_class, redirect.details.cause.code},
  html: {html_class, html.details.cause.code}
}
```

Stop the server explicitly.

```elixir
:ok = ThousandIsland.stop(server)
```

## Safe telemetry

Lab HTTP spans carry the method and the profile; URLs, headers and bodies
never become labels.

```elixir
drain = fn drain ->
  receive do
    {:wotex_lab_telemetry, event, measurements, metadata} ->
      [{event, measurements, metadata} | drain.(drain)]
  after
    0 -> []
  end
end

spans = drain.(drain)
:telemetry.detach(telemetry_id)

http_stops =
  for {[:wotex, :lab, :http, :request, :stop], _measurements, metadata} <- spans, do: metadata

labels_safe? =
  http_stops != [] and not (inspect(spans) =~ "127.0.0.1") and not (inspect(spans) =~ "21.5")

{Enum.uniq(http_stops), labels_safe?}
```

## Expected output

| Check | Expectation |
| --- | --- |
| `read-through-runtime` | `readproperty` result with payload `21.5` and HTTP status 200 |
| `read-through-client` | The client returns status 200 and the body `21.5` |
| `oversized-refused` | A 4 KiB body over a 1 KiB bound is `:unavailable` with cause `:client_request_failed` |
| `deadline-refused` | A 400 ms response under a 100 ms deadline is class `:timeout` |
| `redirect-refused` | A 302 is `:permanent` with cause `:http_status`; nothing follows it |
| `media-type-refused` | `text/html` for a JSON Property is `:protocol` with cause `:unexpected_response_media_type` |

```elixir
%{
  "read-through-runtime" =>
    runtime_result.operation == :readproperty and runtime_result.payload == 21.5 and
      runtime_result.metadata.http.status == 200,
  "read-through-client" => direct_status == 200 and direct.body == "21.5",
  "oversized-refused" =>
    oversized_class == :unavailable and oversized.details.cause.code == :client_request_failed,
  "deadline-refused" => slow_class == :timeout,
  "redirect-refused" => redirect_class == :permanent and redirect.details.cause.code == :http_status,
  "media-type-refused" =>
    html_class == :protocol and html.details.cause.code == :unexpected_response_media_type and
      labels_safe?
}
```

## Public seam

`http.client` — `Wotex.Binding.HTTP.Client.request/3`, `subscribe/4`,
`close/2` (consumer-implements; the Lab reference is `ReqClient`).
`runtime.credentials` — `Wotex.Runtime.Credentials.resolve/4` (the `NoSec`
adapter here).

## Spec and completion IDs

* Lab: WLB.04 (`WLB-C04`, `WLB-C08`).
* Upstream consumer evidence: `RT-C04`, `WBH-C04`.

## Replacement adapter

Implement `Wotex.Binding.HTTP.Client` with your own HTTP library: `request/3`
receives a `Wotex.Binding.HTTP.Request` and the resolved credential, must
honour `deadline/1` and `max_response_bytes/1`, must not follow redirects on
its own, and returns `Wotex.Binding.HTTP.Response.new/3`. Pass it as
`client: {YourClient, config}` to `Wotex.Binding.HTTP.config/1`. The binding
keeps status and media-type mapping, so your client never interprets WoT
semantics.
